## 4. Logical Relationships

| From → To | Relation | Runtime | Justification |
|-----------|----------|---------|---------------|
| **G1 → G2_3, G4** | OR | `FALLBACK(G2_3, G4)` | Mission can be carried out by a single robot (G2_3) or by multiple robots in parallel (G4). Fallback ensures the alternative is used if the first fails. |
| **G2_3 → G2, G3** | AND | `;` | Collection must finish before delivery starts. |
| **G2 → G2.1, G2.2, G2.3, G2.4** | AND | `;` | Each sub‑step of collection must be performed in order. |
| **G2.4 → AT4, AT5** | OR | `FALLBACK(AT4, AT5)` | Battery‑low (AT5) vs. normal operation (AT4) are mutually exclusive alternatives. |
| **G3 → G3.1, G3.2, G3.3** | AND | `;` | Delivery consists of transport, battery check, and failure handling. |
| **G3.2 → AT7, AT8** | OR | `FALLBACK(AT7, AT8)` | Battery‑low (AT7) vs. normal operation (AT8). |
| **G4 → AT10, AT11** | AND | `#` | Parallel assignment and execution of multiple robots to reduce mission time. |

---

### Final Remarks

All goals are classified as **Perform** because the mission requires concrete actions (moving, requesting, waiting, reporting).  
The runtime annotations (sequential `;`, parallel `#`, and fallback `FALLBACK`) are chosen directly from the mission description: collection must precede delivery, parallel execution reduces time, and battery‑low conditions trigger alternative plans.  
Task locations and robot counts are justified from the description (storage, recharge station, checkpoint, command center, etc.).  

This goal model can be directly used to generate a hierarchical task network or to drive a multi‑robot planning system.
